WHAT A SURVEY OF THESE REPOSITORIES REPORTED. These are LEADS, not settled facts.

A single pass swept the whole estate with limited tools, and it has been wrong: on
2026-08-08 it reported that a repository contained no reference to a package that its
own source uses in twenty files, and a roster was minted on that. Treat every line
below as what the survey BELIEVES, to be confirmed by the investigator that owns the
codeline. Do NOT restate any of it in a brief as established, verified, or confirmed —
a brief is inherited whole and re-checked by nothing, so a wrong lead written as a fact
becomes an instruction.

Propose roles for the codelines the work appears to REACH. A codeline reported
no_work_found is not confirmed clear, and one reported not_investigated or failed was
not established either way — do not treat any of the three as proof about that repo:

- mocka: in_scope — areas: src/fares.ts, test/fares.test.ts
    files it opened: src/fares.ts, test/fares.test.ts, package.json
    evidence: Opened src/fares.ts: fareFor() checks `if (rider.age > 65) return CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS;` — a rider aged exactly 65 falls through to the `return BASE_FARE_CENTS` at the end, matching MOCK3-1's description of age-65 riders being charged 350 instead of 175 while age 66+ (age > 65) is correct. test/fares.test.ts has a case for age 70 (well past the boundary) but no case at exactly age 65, consistent with the bug going unnoticed. package.json confirms vitest + typescript devDependencies matching the declared manifest. No stop/schedule/formatting code exists in this codeline.
- mockb: in_scope — areas: src/schedule.ts, test/schedule.test.ts
    files it opened: src/schedule.ts, test/schedule.test.ts, package.json
    evidence: Opened src/schedule.ts: formatStops() loops `for (let i = 0; i < stops.length - 1; i++)`, which excludes the final stop from the rendered output — matching MOCK3-2's description that a three-stop line shows only two. totalMinutes() reads `stops[stops.length - 1].minutes` directly (not via the loop), so it correctly reports the final stop's time, matching the ticket's note that journey length is unaffected. test/schedule.test.ts only asserts the string contains '->' and contains the first stop name ('Union'), never checking for the last stop ('Eglinton'), consistent with the omission going unnoticed. package.json confirms vitest + typescript devDependencies matching the declared manifest. No fare/rider code exists in this codeline.

SEPARATELY — and this is a recommendation about the TEAM, not something discovered
about the code: a survey of the estate suggests these codelines need their own
investigator. Propose one per codeline named here, with the stated focus:

- mocka: focus on The age-boundary condition in the fare calculation and its test coverage for the exact age-65 case (because fareFor()'s boundary check uses a strict comparison that excludes age 65 from the concession fare, and no existing test exercises age 65 exactly)
- mockb: focus on The stop-iteration bound in the departure board rendering and its test coverage for the last stop's presence in output (because formatStops()'s loop bound excludes the final stop, and no existing test checks that the last stop name appears in the rendered output)

AND — also a recommendation about the TEAM — the survey reports these codelines need
